
************ S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)***************
Meaning

A class should have only one reason to change.

One class = one responsibility.

BAD CODE : 
class Report
{
public:
    void generateReport()
    {
        cout << "Generating report\n";
    }

    void saveToFile()
    {
        cout << "Saving report to file\n";
    }
};

Problem

Report has 2 responsibilities:

Generate report
Save report

If file-saving changes → class changes.

GOOD EXAMPLE:
class Report
{
public:
    void generateReport()
    {
        cout << "Generating report\n";
    }
};

class ReportSaver
{
public:
    void saveToFile(Report& report)
    {
        cout << "Saving report to file\n";
    }
};

Interview Line

A class should do only one thing and have only one reason to change.

***********2. O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)******
Meaning

Software should be:

Open for extension
Closed for modification

Add new behavior without changing old code.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// BAD DESIGN (Violates Open/Closed Principle)
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

class PaymentProcessor
{
public:
    void pay(int type)
    {
        if(type == 1)
        {
            cout << "UPI Payment" << endl;
        }
        else if(type == 2)
        {
            cout << "Card Payment" << endl;
        }
    }
};

int main()
{
    PaymentProcessor p;

    p.pay(1);
    p.pay(2);

    // Later...
    // Add NetBanking?
    //
    // Need to modify PaymentProcessor
    //
    // else if(type == 3)
    //      cout << "NetBanking Payment";
}

Problems
Tight coupling
Violates Open/Closed Principle
Every new payment type modifies existing code
Needs recompilation and retesting

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Payment Interface
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

class Payment
{
public:
    virtual void pay() = 0;
    virtual ~Payment() = default;
};

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Concrete Payments
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

class UPIPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "UPI Payment" << endl;
    }
};

class CardPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "Card Payment" << endl;
    }
};

class NetBankingPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "NetBanking Payment" << endl;
    }
};

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Payment Processor
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

class PaymentProcessor
{
public:
    void processPayment(Payment* payment)
    {
        payment->pay();
    }
};

//////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// Client
//////////////////////////////////////////////////////

int main()
{
    PaymentProcessor processor;

    UPIPayment upi;
    CardPayment card;
    NetBankingPayment net;

    processor.processPayment(&upi);
    processor.processPayment(&card);
    processor.processPayment(&net);

    return 0;
}

Need:
Avoid modifying existing code whenever new functionality is added.*******

How OCP helps:
Depend on abstractions (Payment)
Extend by adding new derived classes
Existing code remains unchanged

Interview Line
Use abstraction/polymorphism to extend behavior without modifying existing code.

**********************************L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)*****************************************
Meaning
Derived class should be replaceable with base class without breaking behavior.
In simple words:

If B is derived from A, then anywhere an A object is expected, a B object should work correctly.

👉 If Dog is a child of Animal, then wherever Animal is used, Dog should work correctly.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Vehicle {
public:
    virtual void refuel() {
        cout << "Refueling vehicle\n";
    }
};

class PetrolCar : public Vehicle {
};

class ElectricCar : public Vehicle {
public:
    void refuel() override {
        throw runtime_error("Electric car doesn't use fuel");
    }
};

void fuelVehicle(Vehicle* v) {
    v->refuel();
}

int main() {
    PetrolCar p;
    ElectricCar e;

    fuelVehicle(&p); // OK
    fuelVehicle(&e); // Problem
}

Why issue?
Base class vehicle says:
Every vehicle can refuel().

But electriccar cannot truly refuel.
It changes expected behavior.

but if someone writes 
fuelVehicle(&e); // Problem

they expect normal refueling behavior from base contract.
But derived class breaks that expectation.
That is an LSP violation

******INTERVIEW LINE****
Interview Explanation
Parent class promised:
All vehicles can refuel

But child class failed to support it.
So derived object cannot replace base object safely.

→ LSP violated.

Interview explanation (important):

You can say:
Base class should represent behavior common to all derived classes.
Here refuel() is not applicable to electric cars.
So electriccar should not inherit a contract requiring refuel().

***Better design:

Not every vehicle should have refuel().

Separate behaviors.
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class vehicle
{
public:
    virtual void start()
    {
        cout << "start vehicle" << endl;
    }
};

class fuelvehicle : public vehicle
{
public:
    virtual void refuel()
    {
        cout << "refuel vehicle" << endl;
    }
};

class petrolcar : public fuelvehicle
{
};

class electriccar : public vehicle
{
};

void processcar(fuelvehicle* p)
{
    p->refuel();
}

int main()
{
    petrolcar p1;
    processcar(&p1);

    electriccar e1;
    // processcar(&e1); ❌ not allowed

    return 0;
}

This is proper LSP.

Because:

Only fuel vehicles support refuel()
Electric cars are not forced to implement invalid behavior

This type of example is very interview-friendly because it is:

simple
realistic
clearly shows wrong inheritance design.

***********4. I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)**********************************
Meaning
Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.
Instead of one large interface, create multiple smaller, focused interfaces.

Interview one-liner:
"Prefer many small interfaces over one large interface."

***BAD DESIGN***
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Payment
{
public:
    virtual void pay() = 0;
    virtual void refund() = 0;
};

class UPIPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "UPI Payment\n";
    }

    void refund() override
    {
        // UPI app doesn't support refund
        throw runtime_error("Refund not supported");
    }
};

class CardPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "Card Payment\n";
    }

    void refund() override
    {
        cout << "Refund processed\n";
    }
};
Problem
UPIPayment is forced to implement refund().
It doesn't actually support it.
Interface is too large.
Classes implement methods they don't need.

ISP violated.
**GOOD CODE**
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Payment
{
public:
    virtual void pay() = 0;
};

class Refund
{
public:
    virtual void refund() = 0;
};

class UPIPayment : public Payment
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "UPI Payment\n";
    }
};

class CardPayment : public Payment, public Refund
{
public:
    void pay() override
    {
        cout << "Card Payment\n";
    }

    void refund() override
    {
        cout << "Refund processed\n";
    }
};

Why is this better?
Small, focused interfaces.
Classes implement only what they need.
No dummy or empty implementations.
Easier to maintain and extend.

Interview Explanation

Bad Design:

Payment
 ├── pay()
 └── refund()

UPI implements refund() even though it doesn't need it.

Good Design:

Payment
 └── pay()

Refund
 └── refund()

CardPayment → Payment + Refund
UPIPayment  → Payment

Each class depends only on the functionality it actually uses.

Interview One-Liner

"ISP says a class should not be forced to implement methods it doesn't need. 
Instead of one large interface, split it into smaller, focused interfaces."



# 5. D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

## Meaning

**High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules.**

Both should depend on **abstractions (interfaces).**

**Interview One-liner:**

> High-level classes should depend on interfaces, not concrete implementations.

---

## Real-Life Example

**Notification System**

Notification can send messages through:

* Email
* SMS
* WhatsApp
* Push Notification

The Notification class should not care **how** the message is sent.

---

# BAD DESIGN (Violates DIP)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class EmailService
{
public:
    void sendMessage()
    {
        cout << "Email sent\n";
    }
};

class Notification
{
private:
    EmailService email;      // Direct dependency

public:
    void notify()
    {
        email.sendMessage();
    }
};

int main()
{
    Notification n;
    n.notify();
}
```

### Problems

* Notification directly depends on `EmailService`.
* Tight coupling between high-level and low-level classes.
* Difficult to switch to:

  * SMS
  * WhatsApp
  * Push Notification
* Every new message service requires modifying `Notification`.
* Violates **Dependency Inversion Principle**.
* Also violates **Open/Closed Principle**.

---

# GOOD DESIGN (Follows DIP)

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Abstraction
class MessageService
{
public:
    virtual void sendMessage() = 0;
    virtual ~MessageService() = default;
};

// Low-level module
class EmailService : public MessageService
{
public:
    void sendMessage() override
    {
        cout << "Email sent\n";
    }
};

// Low-level module
class SMSService : public MessageService
{
public:
    void sendMessage() override
    {
        cout << "SMS sent\n";
    }
};

// High-level module
class Notification
{
private:
    MessageService* service;

public:
    Notification(MessageService* s)
        : service(s)
    {
    }

    void notify()
    {
        service->sendMessage();
    }
};

int main()
{
    EmailService email;
    Notification n1(&email);
    n1.notify();

    SMSService sms;
    Notification n2(&sms);
    n2.notify();

    return 0;
}
```

---

# Why This Design Is Better

* Notification depends only on the `MessageService` interface.
* Easy to add new services:

  * WhatsAppService
  * PushNotificationService
  * SlackService
* No changes required in `Notification`.
* Loose coupling.
* Easy to test using mock implementations.
* Follows both **DIP** and **Open/Closed Principle**.

---

# Interview Explanation

```
High-Level Module
-----------------
Notification

        |
        v

Abstraction
-----------
MessageService

     /        \
    /          \
EmailService  SMSService

Low-Level Modules
```

* **High-Level Module:** `Notification`
* **Low-Level Modules:** `EmailService`, `SMSService`
* **Abstraction:** `MessageService`

The high-level module now depends only on the abstraction, not on concrete implementations.

---

# Real-World Use Cases

* Notification Service → Email, SMS, Push Notification
* Payment System → UPI, Card, PayPal
* Logging Framework → Console Logger, File Logger, Database Logger
* Database Layer → MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle

---

# Interview One-Line Answer

> "Dependency Inversion Principle states that high-level modules should depend on abstractions 
rather than concrete implementations. 
This reduces coupling, improves extensibility, and makes the system easier to test."

---

# Memory Trick

**Bad:**

```
Notification
      |
      v
 EmailService
```

High-level class depends on concrete implementation ❌

---

**Good:**

```
Notification
      |
      v
MessageService
   /        \
Email      SMS
```

High-level class depends on an interface ✔

These notes are concise, technically correct, and are at the level expected in senior C++/LLD interviews.



